A||||13||GodSend||Name that bolt!?||||5||
Z||000000||GodSend||08-21-2001||02:13 PM||||Easy question guys.  Ive been meaning to fix my squeeky alt belt for awhile.  It seems pretty tight so i went out and bought a replacement one today.  ive looked though the haynes manual as well as online, and everyone says to simple loosen the "adjustment bolt" and tilt the alt forward.  Easy enough, except I am not sure which bolt that is.  I have supplied a few pictures, could someone be kind enough to circle the correct bolt so I can finish this job, thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.globalserve.net/~kesik/alt1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.globalserve.net/~kesik/alt2.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.globalserve.net/~kesik/alt3.jpg"> <P><BR>p.s. the bushings on my dogbone look pretty pathetic too - winter project.  Any recommendations?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||JayUSA77||08-21-2001||02:16 PM||||get the bushings from the fiero store. It will be cheaper than buying a new dog bone and will work great.<BR>j<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||08-21-2001||02:18 PM||||You're looking in the wrong place  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The adjustment bolt is underneath the car, on the bottom of the alternator. The best access, I've found, is to pull the passenger-rear wheel off, pop the 2 rivets holding the shroud, and bend it back. It's the peice with the long hole slotted to fit the control arm.  After that, you should be able to see it clearly. It's a 13mm bolt going into a  long "C" shaped slot.||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||08-21-2001||02:25 PM||||This alt bracket is broken but you can see the bolt you need to loosen.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P8210006.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||GodSend||08-21-2001||03:48 PM||||Super, thanks guys.  I managed to locate the bolt, and loosen it, but the alt will not move no matter how much I push, or pull  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Any otehr suggestions?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||08-21-2001||03:53 PM||||Grab a set of adjustable pliers and grab onto the bolt and the end of the bracket. then squeeze HARD. That'll tighten it up!<BR>Then tighten the bolt, repeat. That'll take up some more slack.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000006||Mach10||08-21-2001||03:56 PM||||Oh, sorry, you want to loosen? Give the joints a shot of WD-40, then give the alternator a couple of taps with a hammer. For best results (and no cracking of alternator) try to do it on the adjuster bolt, or the boss just behind it. Remember, a few light taps vs. one big thwack. If it's too awkward, do it on the casing, but BE CAREFUL. Otherwise you will have 2 complete alternators, neither of which will work. <P>EDIT:<BR>also, I've done it by prying between the adjuster bolt and the slot. This will only work if the adjustment is currently nearer the end. Once it "breaks" free, it should be fairly easy.<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Leadfoot||08-21-2001||04:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GodSend:<BR><B>p.s. the bushings on my dogbone look pretty pathetic too - winter project.  Any recommendations?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Better check your tranny and engine mounts.  If you dogbone bushings were like that for a while the mounts might be damaged.  Also, you said you can't budge the alternator after loosening the bolt.  Did you also loosen the bolt and nut on the top of the alt.  If not, you will have a heck of a time moving the alt.||||reg||1
Z||000008||pherder||08-21-2001||04:07 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by skitime:<BR><B>This alt bracket is broken but you can see the bolt you need to loosen.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/alternator.jpg">   </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you still can't move the alternator, loosen the <B><FONT SIZE="3">TOP</FONT></B> bolt which is located on the other side of the alternator.  13 mm on my two.<P>Do this first before hitting the alterantor with anything other than your hand!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 08-21-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000009||Mach10||08-21-2001||04:30 PM||||The top bolt never was a problem for me. I just coated it with WD-40, used a hammer to loosen it (2 taps) then I just grabbed both sides of the belt and squeezed. Slipped the belt off, replaced my water pump, threw on a new belt, and spent the better part of 3 hours figuring out how to tension it.<P>BTW: About 1 week after you replace your belt, re-tension it, or else it'll start squealing again...<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||GodSend||08-21-2001||05:49 PM||||All done, no noise.  Thanks guys.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||08-21-2001||06:00 PM||||No problem. Like I said, don't forget to retension the old belt after a week. Earlier if it starts to chirp.||||reg||6
Z||000012||rogergarrison||08-21-2001||09:54 PM||||just a tip when i first replaced my alternator. the pivot bolt (the big one at top) was siezed in the bracket because of the two different type metals. had to take off whole front bracket and torch it out. I replaced the whole bolt with a stainless one from hardware store and nut with washers. I got a bolt the next size down in diameter too so it wont ever happen again.||||reg||1
Z||000013||skitime||08-21-2001||10:08 PM||||That is why the bracket go broken the top bolt was seized.  Good advise||||reg||1
